   "t": "Один спрятался под столом.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Не стойте просто так, ищите!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я сейчас его поймаю.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Back through the chaos, slowly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Блин, блин, блин, блин.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Damn, damn, damn, damn.' Now we can examine the dialogue: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Блин",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is a pancake. It is a mild 'damn' that Russians use because it sounds like a stronger word and is safe. Max says it four times.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я уронил коробку на пол.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I dropped the box onto the floor.' Let's inspect this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "уронил",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'dropped', or 'made fall', and it is a man's past action. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "на",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "пол",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'onto the floor', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "на",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'on' with a direction.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Они очень быстро бегают.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'They run very fast.' Russian splits 'run' in two. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "бегают",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the running-around kind, here and there, not the one-direction kind.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Что ты опять натворил, Макс?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What did you do again, Max?' We can look at the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "натворил",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'got up to', for trouble. It's built on the word for 'to create', so Barinov asks what Max has 'created' this time. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "опять",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'again'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я просто хотел взять продукты.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I just wanted to take the products.' We will analyze the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "продукты",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " looks like 'products' but means groceries, any food. His 'wanted' picks up a sound because he's a man.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Быстро берите все сети!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Quickly get all the nets!' Orders to a crowd take a special ending, so it's ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "берите",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". ",
